1
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:09,360
And now the snakes are on the alert.

2
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:15,440
This is the best feeding opportunity
they will get all year.

3
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:34,280
On flat ground, a baby iguana
can outrun a racer snake.

4
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:39,520
But others are waiting in ambush.

5
00:01:05,199 --> 00:01:11,840
Another hatchling has its first glimpse
of a dangerous world.

6
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:11,440
A snake's eyes aren't very good.

7
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:15,440
But they can detect movement.

8
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:21,000
So, if the hatchling keeps its nerve,
it may just avoid detection.

9
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:55,080
A near escape.

10
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:03,540
The lucky survivors could begin learning

11
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:08,540
the unique way of life demanded
by this hostile island.

12
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:12,540
As they mature, young males
begin to explore

13
00:04:12,540 --> 00:04:17,200
the boundaries of the pride's territory.

14
00:04:20,799 --> 00:04:25,080
Red has ventured out alone.

15
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:44,240
And blunders straight into the middle
of the hyena clan.

16
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:09,240
He's trapped by over 20 of them.

17
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,700
The pack tries to wear him down.

18
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:33,480
This number of hyenas could kill him.

19
00:05:58,040 --> 00:06:02,520
It's impossible to fight them
all at once.

20
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:19,560
He can't keep them at bay much longer.

21
00:06:19,560 --> 00:06:22,960
He's tiring fast.

22
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:35,800
His ally, Tortue, has heard
the commotion.

23
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:59,120
Now, the odds have changed.

24
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:12,240
Even for twenty hyenas, a pair of
male lions is too much to take on.

25
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:28,220
During the dry season,
over half a million terns crowd

26
00:07:28,220 --> 00:07:32,280
onto this remote atoll
in the Indian Ocean.

27
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:43,480
Their chicks are still
in their dark juvenile plumage.

28
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:50,120
They vary in age.

29
00:07:56,480 --> 00:08:00,480
Whilst the more advanced chicks
take to the air,

30
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:04,480
others aren't quite ready yet.

31
00:08:29,919 --> 00:08:33,419
Those just starting to learn to fly

32
00:08:33,419 --> 00:08:36,919
use the shallow lagoon that occupies

33
00:08:36,919 --> 00:08:41,919
the center of the atoll
as their training ground.

34
00:08:41,919 --> 00:08:46,400
It's difficult for some of them
to stay aloft for long.

35
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:11,839
Giant trevallies.

36
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:26,140
Usually, they are solitary hunters,

37
00:09:26,140 --> 00:09:32,140
but about 50 of them have come here
from neighboring reefs

38
00:09:32,140 --> 00:09:35,560
attracted by this abundance
of potential prey.

39
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:46,800
The fledglings stay out of the water
if they can.

40
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:50,840
They even drink on the wing.

41
00:10:06,760 --> 00:10:13,960
If the trevally are to catch one now,
they have to up their game.

42
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:27,840
So, there is a fish here that amazingly
has a brain capable of calculating

43
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:34,440
the air speed, altitude, and trajectory
of a bird.

44
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:07,680
The time comes when every fledgling

45
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:11,640
has to take to the air
and collect food for itself.

46
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:38,960
Their parents lead them
to the training grounds.

47
00:13:11,800 --> 00:13:17,839
If they are to survive,
they must learn quickly.

48
00:13:24,400 --> 00:13:28,600
After a month of practicing
over the lagoon,

49
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:30,900
the youngsters start to leave

50
00:13:30,900 --> 00:13:34,400
and take their chances out
over the open sea.

51
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:39,440
Exhausted from his swim,
the bear must regain his strength.

52
00:13:44,520 --> 00:13:50,020
The next day, a sea fog
shrouds the island.

53
00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:54,520
The walruses sense that
they're in danger.

54
00:13:54,520 --> 00:14:00,560
Using the fog as cover,
the bear approaches the herd.

55
00:14:03,720 --> 00:14:07,420
The adults close ranks
around their young,

56
00:14:07,420 --> 00:14:11,360
presenting a wall of blubber
and hide.

57
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:22,600
He tests the barrier,
but it stands firm.

58
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:29,880
It appears that the world's largest
land carnivore has met his match.

59
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:40,080
There must be a chink
in the armor somewhere.

60
00:14:42,520 --> 00:14:45,240
Not here.

61
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:57,640
This female walrus is shielding her pup.

62
00:14:57,640 --> 00:15:04,040
If he can just prize her off,

63
00:15:04,040 --> 00:15:10,880
the bear's paws and teeth
can't penetrate her thick hide.

64
00:15:17,680 --> 00:15:23,280
With the herd retreating to water,
the bear must move quickly.

65
00:15:27,080 --> 00:15:32,200
Having failed with one,
he heads straight for another.

66
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:53,400
The chance of his first meal in months
is slipping away.

67
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:03,200
He seems increasingly desperate.

68
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:10,600
It's now or never.

69
00:16:10,600 --> 00:16:15,120
He must avoid the stabbing tusks
if he's to win.

70
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:23,520
The flailing walrus
is immensely powerful

71
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:26,520
and drags the bear away
from the shallows

72
00:16:26,520 --> 00:16:30,040
towards the safety of the herd.

73
00:16:43,640 --> 00:16:47,560
It slips from his grasp.

74
00:16:50,440 --> 00:16:59,240
Mantids will eat anything that moves,
including other mantids.

75
00:17:12,760 --> 00:17:16,360
Time to leave.

76
00:17:25,199 --> 00:17:29,199
This tiny insect is now
open to attack

77
00:17:29,199 --> 00:17:33,320
from predators lurking
in the undergrowth.

78
00:17:40,920 --> 00:17:48,840
Whether an individual mantis survives
or not is partly a matter of chance.

79
00:17:50,760 --> 00:17:54,760
Whether it's spotted by a predator.

80
00:17:59,600 --> 00:18:05,240
Whether it turns right or left.

81
00:18:12,680 --> 00:18:17,480
So far, its luck has held.

82
00:18:17,480 --> 00:18:22,440
But this hungry jumping spider
is still in pursuit.

83
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:29,820
A mantis is born with
exceptional eyesight.

84
00:18:29,820 --> 00:18:34,240
But the spider's is even better.

85
00:18:37,520 --> 00:18:41,820
Although this young mantis
can't yet fly,

86
00:18:41,820 --> 00:18:46,320
its long forelegs evolved
to catch prey give it reach.

87
00:19:12,840 --> 00:19:16,960
There seems to be no escape.

88
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:28,120
But this mantis has a surprising line
in self-defense.

89
00:19:28,120 --> 00:19:33,560
Kung fu, praying mantis style.

90
00:19:44,120 --> 00:19:47,720
Of course, it's all bluff.

91
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:51,360
Trying to look bigger
and confuse its enemy.

92
00:19:52,880 --> 00:19:56,280
But it's got away with it.

93
00:20:03,560 --> 00:20:07,560
Just staying alive
for its first few hours

94
00:20:07,560 --> 00:20:11,560
is a significant accomplishment
for a newly hatched insect.

95
00:20:11,560 --> 00:20:15,060
But there's still a long way to go.

96
00:20:15,060 --> 00:20:19,560
With a bit of luck,
in 2 months' time,

97
00:20:19,560 --> 00:20:24,120
it will be as big and beautiful
as this orchid mantid.

98
00:20:34,080 --> 00:20:37,880
Or maybe not.

99
00:20:37,880 --> 00:20:42,560
After all, mantids are cannibals.

100
00:20:48,960 --> 00:20:54,160
Stony canyons provide a sanctuary
for animals throughout the Middle East.

101
00:20:54,160 --> 00:20:58,840
Like Nubian ibex.

102
00:21:04,800 --> 00:21:08,600
These wild goats can only survive here

103
00:21:08,600 --> 00:21:13,360
because precious water bubbles
to the surface in springs.

104
00:21:16,280 --> 00:21:22,080
In summer, the females come to drink
at these pools every day.

105
00:21:22,080 --> 00:21:26,880
The pool is guarded by a male ibex.

106
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:31,760
He has exclusive access to the females
who come here.

107
00:21:34,520 --> 00:21:39,760
He tastes the air to see
if any of them are ready to mate.

108
00:21:43,520 --> 00:21:51,120
But this is such a prized spot
that inevitably he has competition.

109
00:21:54,520 --> 00:21:58,360
Getting the upper ground will be key.

110
00:22:24,440 --> 00:22:30,080
The males' skulls are specially
reinforced to take a battering.

111
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:52,560
They're evenly matched.

112
00:22:52,560 --> 00:22:55,520
The battle could last up to an hour.

113
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:22,680
But when a third male joins in,
no one seems to know who to fight.

114
00:23:25,800 --> 00:23:31,280
The party is soon broken up
by a telltale smell in the air.

115
00:23:39,480 --> 00:23:44,680
The ibex are right to be skittish.

116
00:23:44,680 --> 00:23:50,000
Bedouin have always been
skilled hunters.

117
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:56,160
Kenya, famous for its big cats,
the supreme hunters.

118
00:24:07,080 --> 00:24:10,880
Cheetahs specialize in hunting at speed.

119
00:24:10,880 --> 00:24:16,080
Though fast, they're fragile creatures
built to sprint after small prey.

120
00:24:16,080 --> 00:24:24,080
They don't have the strength or weight
of a lion to bring down larger animals.

121
00:24:24,080 --> 00:24:26,480
This male is different.

122
00:24:26,480 --> 00:24:28,880
He doesn't hunt alone.

123
00:24:28,880 --> 00:24:32,680
He's learned that there is strength
in numbers.

124
00:24:44,880 --> 00:24:50,080
But here, there are not just two,
but three cheetahs.

125
00:24:50,080 --> 00:24:53,440
A band of brothers.

126
00:24:58,560 --> 00:25:02,060
They have changed their tactics
and by doing so

127
00:25:02,060 --> 00:25:05,560
have taken their prey
by surprise.

128
00:25:05,560 --> 00:25:12,720
They have learned that working together
they can bring down large prey.

129
00:25:15,600 --> 00:25:22,100
An ostrich, a bird that towers over a
cheetah and is more than twice as heavy.

130
00:25:22,100 --> 00:25:30,100
It can't fly to escape danger,
but it can lash out with a deadly kick.

131
00:25:30,100 --> 00:25:35,280
A female unaware as yet of any danger.

132
00:25:39,720 --> 00:25:44,520
Even with three of them,
this is still highly risky.

133
00:25:44,520 --> 00:25:50,000
If one gets injured, the other two
couldn't hope to tackle such large prey.

134
00:25:56,720 --> 00:26:03,520
On the other hand, if they get it right,
the rewards are huge.

135
00:26:09,960 --> 00:26:14,460
The male has spotted
one of the brothers, but only one.

136
00:26:14,460 --> 00:26:17,480
It's not too worried.

137
00:26:28,760 --> 00:26:32,440
Then suddenly, there are three.

138
00:26:42,360 --> 00:26:48,640
The female is slower to realize the
danger, and the cheetahs switch targets.

139
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:16,800
It takes the combined effort
and weight of all three

140
00:27:16,800 --> 00:27:20,000
brothers to bring down
this powerful bird.

141
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:24,480
Even now, the ostrich
could land a fatal kick.

142
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:34,980
So far, the brothers are winning.

143
00:27:34,980 --> 00:27:39,830
Ostriches have yet to find a way
to foil such tactics.

144
00:27:39,830 --> 00:27:42,880
High up in the Rockies,
one of the greatest

145
00:27:42,880 --> 00:27:45,560
gatherings in the wild west
is underway.

146
00:27:49,600 --> 00:27:56,600
For most of the year, bison bulls
and cows live separate lives.

147
00:27:56,600 --> 00:28:00,600
But for a few weeks
in late summer,

148
00:28:00,600 --> 00:28:04,600
thousands congregate
in this Yellowstone Valley.

149
00:28:04,600 --> 00:28:08,600
It's the rut, the mating season,

150
00:28:08,600 --> 00:28:12,600
and the biggest bulls are busy
guarding their harems.

151
00:28:12,600 --> 00:28:18,080
A dominant bull might mate
with over 50 females.

152
00:28:22,480 --> 00:28:29,160
For the calves born this year, now
is the time to stretch their young legs.

153
00:28:38,240 --> 00:28:44,080
But as the herd grows,
so tensions rise.

154
00:29:22,520 --> 00:29:27,520
The rut takes a heavy toll
on the big bulls.

155
00:29:27,520 --> 00:29:33,120
So, arriving now could give
this young contender an edge.

156
00:29:41,640 --> 00:29:46,200
But, there's life in the old bull yet.

157
00:30:09,160 --> 00:30:15,160
Head to head, the challenger
more than holds his own.

158
00:30:15,160 --> 00:30:19,660
Then, he makes a small mistake.

159
00:30:19,660 --> 00:30:25,280
Shunted aside, his challenge
is brutally ended.

160
00:30:41,160 --> 00:30:48,440
The young bull gambled everything
and has paid a heavy price.

161
00:30:54,600 --> 00:30:59,000
Somehow, in this melee
of 60,000 or so penguins,

162
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:03,040
a parent has to find its chick.

163
00:31:11,400 --> 00:31:15,200
It returns to the place
where it last left its chick

164
00:31:15,200 --> 00:31:18,720
in the hope that it might
still be close by.

165
00:31:20,280 --> 00:31:23,380
But, chicks tend to wander.

166
00:31:23,380 --> 00:31:26,880
So, the adult has to call to it.

167
00:31:39,600 --> 00:31:45,100
The chick responds,
and they slowly home in on one another.

168
00:31:45,100 --> 00:31:47,700
The plaintive entreaties of the chick

169
00:31:47,700 --> 00:31:51,440
stimulates the adult to regurgitate
a mouthful of fish.

170
00:32:00,440 --> 00:32:03,740
With the return of one parent,
the other is free

171
00:32:03,740 --> 00:32:07,640
to go to sea to feed for itself.

172
00:32:20,640 --> 00:32:24,040
Aware of the leopard seal's presence,

173
00:32:24,040 --> 00:32:27,440
the penguins press together
at the ice edge,

174
00:32:27,440 --> 00:32:30,880
unwilling to be the first
to risk diving in.

175
00:32:45,080 --> 00:32:51,880
Occasionally, the seal comes out
onto the ice and attempts to grab one.

176
00:33:05,080 --> 00:33:11,640
But, its most successful strategy by far
is to lie in wait.

177
00:33:17,400 --> 00:33:21,900
It hides behind the corner of ice.

178
00:33:21,900 --> 00:33:27,800
The emperors gain confidence
and make a dash for it.

179
00:33:38,200 --> 00:33:43,200
The first wave of penguins escape.

180
00:33:43,200 --> 00:33:47,800
Once in open water, they'll be safe.

181
00:33:50,920 --> 00:33:54,720
But, the seal is alerted
by the noise

182
00:33:54,720 --> 00:33:58,520
and through the mass of bubbles,
it makes its attack.

183
00:34:07,080 --> 00:34:11,919
Almost invariably, it makes a kill.

184
00:34:17,960 --> 00:34:21,460
Encouraged by the absence
of the seal,

185
00:34:21,460 --> 00:34:25,200
the remaining penguins make a break
for the open sea.

186
00:34:42,639 --> 00:34:45,839
In time, their chicks will fledge.

187
00:34:45,839 --> 00:34:50,339
And when the Antarctic autumn
is near its end,

188
00:34:50,339 --> 00:34:54,089
these adults will walk across
the newly formed ice

189
00:34:54,089 --> 00:34:57,840
to endure yet another winter
on the frozen sea.

190
00:36:53,120 --> 00:36:55,200
♪ ♪
